## Problem Statement

Capturing a browser tab into a Markdown vault is easy; preserving what was captured while safely deciding where it belongs is not. A raw clip may be incomplete, hostile, duplicated, privacy-sensitive, or ambiguous. A folder may look like a category even when it is only an operational location. A model may suggest a useful placement while lacking authority to change semantic truth. Taxonomy changes may rename, split, merge, or move large portions of a vault. Filesystem materialization may lag, collide with user edits, or fail partway through.

mdplace needs an implementation-ready v1 contract for a single user and a single local Markdown vault in which:

- one intentional browser-tab capture can become one durable Captured Tab Note with normalized readable content and verifiable provenance;
- untrusted Capture Candidates cannot become notes, classifications, remote payloads, or published content until mdplace admits them;
- every admitted note has one stable identity independent of URL, content, filename, folder, browser session, and later recapture;
- every note has exactly one current Placement Outcome: one accepted Primary Category or one explicit Unresolved Placement;
- the Category Tree may evolve under user policy without silently rewriting accepted meaning;
- Intelligence Adapters can advise but cannot establish semantic truth or cause filesystem effects;
- Folder Projection remains a reversible, truthful materialization rather than a second taxonomy;
- users can review and correct outcomes using Markdown and the CLI without a custom Obsidian plugin;
- semantic history, provenance, recovery, and consumer behavior remain inspectable after crashes, drift, policy changes, and taxonomy revisions; and
- the complete product can be validated through a deterministic conformance pack and an observable disposable-vault workflow.

